<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rio Pongo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rio Pongo is a river estuary on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conakry">Conakry</a>, with many channels and creeks among mangrove swamps. In the 18th and 19th centuries it was a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, where European, American and Euro-African traders bought captives brought down from the interior, including Futa Jallon. Slaving continued there into the 1840s despite British efforts to suppress it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rio-pongo.jpg" alt="Rio Pongo" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Braxmeier &amp;amp; Peter in s · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guinea_Coast_OSM.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
